  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nNot much change from the previous forecast. Expect drier conditions \nto continue through the week outside of isolated seabreeze showers \nand thunderstorms. That is due to weak mid level ridging and a \nslightly drier airmass across S TX. We will likely still see some \nactivity over the Texas waters due to now Hurricane Isaias as it \nmoves north-northeast away from us. We will see drier condition \nacross the region leading to much warmer temperatures across S TX. A \nmid/upper level cut off low is progged to track/develop across West \nTX which would provide upper support for convective development \nacross Mexico on Thu/Fri. Though with the lack of moisture in the \nRio Grande Plains chances will remain very low at this time for \nconvection. Another disturbance looks to creep into the region early \nnext week. This disturbance may have a little more moisture to work \nwith and will bring a low chance of showers and storms to the region \nduring the day. \n\nThe main concern Hurricane Isaias will bring to South Texas will be \nan increased risk of coastal flooding, high rip current risk and \nseas building up to 9-10 feet. Coastal Flood Advisories will likely \nbe needed the Friday/Saturday as Isaias progress its way through the \ngulf. Models have came into agreement that swell periods will \nincrease to 9-10 seconds by Friday afternoon/evening along with seas \nbuilding to 9-10 feet or higher due to a strengthening northeast \nflow over the gulf waters. This will provide sufficient energy to \npush the water up to the dunes. If the winds strengthen to 25-30 \nknots with seas building to around 12 feet, then tide levels at high \ntide could reach 3 feet MSL and a Coastal Flood Warning would be \nneeded. However, chances remain low that this will occur, but will \ncontinue to monitor trends.",
